# TypeScript/JavaScript Standards

## TypeScript Configuration
- Use strict mode with all strict flags enabled
- Enable `noImplicitAny` and `strictNullChecks`
- Use `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` for better type safety
- Configure path mapping for clean imports
- Use ESLint and Prettier for consistent formatting

## Type Definitions
- Define interfaces for all data structures
- Use union types instead of any when possible
- Create custom type guards for runtime type checking
- Export types from a central types directory
- Use generic types for reusable components

## React/Next.js Conventions
- Use functional components with TypeScript
- Define proper prop types with interfaces
- Use custom hooks for stateful logic
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use Next.js App Router for new projects

## Import/Export Standards
- Use named exports over default exports
- Group imports: external packages, internal modules, relative imports
- Use absolute imports with path mapping
- Avoid deep nesting in import paths
- Export types and interfaces explicitly

## Async/Await Patterns
- Always handle promise rejections
- Use proper error boundaries for async operations
- Implement loading states for UI components
- Use proper typing for async functions
- Avoid nested promises in favor of async/await

## State Management
- Use React Context for global state
- Consider Zustand or Redux Toolkit for complex state
- Implement proper state immutability
- Use proper typing for state actions and reducers
- Keep state normalized and flat

## API Integration
- Define API response types
- Use proper error handling for API calls
- Implement request/response interceptors
- Use proper loading and error states
- Validate API responses at runtime